# Words 201-250
R = [
("VRB-201","continue – continued – continued – continuing","Use either 'continue to + verb' or 'continue + -ing', not a mix of both.","CON-TINUE: keep going ('tinue' like 'tenure').","'To be continued…' ends a story episode.","Write a sentence about a hobby you want to continue for years.",("start an activity","keep doing it without stopping","still doing it later"),"continue vs resume","Continue = keep going without a break. Resume = start again after a break."),
("VRB-202","stop – stopped – stopped – stopping","'Stop + -ing' = quit the activity. 'Stop + to' = pause one thing in order to do another.","STOP → STOPPED: double P.","'Stop smoking' = quit. 'Stop to smoke' = pause to have a cigarette. Very different!","Write a sentence about a bad habit you stopped.",("an activity is going on","it ends","nothing is happening"),"stop doing vs stop to do","Stop smoking = no more smoking. Stop to smoke = stop walking or working in order to smoke."),
("VRB-203","pause – paused – paused – pausing","To show purpose, use 'to + verb', not 'for + verb'.","PAUSE has 'PAUS' like the pause button ⏸ on a video.","A short pause while speaking sounds confident, not weak.","Write a sentence about when you pause while reading aloud.",("speak or play","stop for a moment","continue again"),"pause vs stop","Pause = stop for a short time and then continue. Stop can be permanent."),
("VRB-204","resume – resumed – resumed – resuming","'Resume' already means 'start again', so 'to start' repeats the idea.","RE-SUME: 're' = again, 'sume' = take up.","Résumé (with accents) is a CV. The verb re-SUME and the noun RÉ-su-mé are said differently.","Write a sentence about when classes resume after a holiday.",("stop for a break","the break ends","start again from where you stopped"),"resume vs résumé","Resume (re-ZYOOM) = start again. Résumé (REZ-yoo-may) = a CV."),
("VRB-205","repeat – repeated – repeated – repeating","'Repeat' already means 'say again', so 'again' is extra.","RE-PEAT: 're' = again.","'Could you repeat that, please?' is the polite version of 'What?'.","Write a polite sentence asking someone to repeat an instruction.",("say something","say it once more","the listener understands"),"repeat vs revise","Repeat = say or do again. Revise = study again to learn, or change to improve."),
("VRB-206","prevent – prevented – prevented – preventing","Use 'prevent someone FROM + -ing', not 'to + verb'.","PRE-VENT: act BEFORE ('pre') something comes.","'Prevention is better than cure.'","Write a sentence about how you prevent a cold in winter.",("see a problem coming","take action early","the problem never happens"),"prevent vs avoid","Prevent = stop something from happening. Avoid = keep away from it."),
("VRB-207","cause – caused – caused – causing","'Cause' takes a direct object, with no 'for'.","CAUSE → because: the cause is the 'because'.","'Cause' as a noun: 'the cause of the fire'. 'Cause someone to do' = make them do.","Write a sentence about what causes traffic jams in your city.",("something happens","it leads to a result","the result is seen"),"cause vs reason","Cause = what makes something happen (often physical). Reason = why someone did something, or the explanation."),
("VRB-208","affect – affected – affected – affecting","'Affect' is the verb; 'effect' is usually the noun.","A for Action (verb): Affect. E for End result (noun): Effect.","'The rain affected the match' / 'The rain had an effect on the match.'","Write a sentence using both 'affect' and 'effect'.",("a decision is made","it changes things","people feel the results"),"affect vs effect","Affect = verb (to influence). Effect = noun (the result)."),
("VRB-209","influence – influenced – influenced – influencing","As a verb, 'influence' takes a direct object, with no 'on'. The noun takes 'on': 'an influence on me'.","IN-FLUENCE: ideas FLOW IN to change you.","An 'influencer' is a social media person who affects buyers.","Write a sentence about who influenced your career choice.",("hear someone's opinion","think about it","change your decision"),"influence vs affect","Affect = change something in any way. Influence = shape how someone thinks or acts."),
("VRB-210","vary – varied – varied – varying","Use 'vary from X to X' or 'vary between X and Y', not 'between X to X'.","VARY → VARIETY: many different kinds.","'Prices may vary' is common on menus and product labels.","Write a sentence about how weather varies across India.",("look at one shop","look at another","the prices are different"),"vary vs very","Vary (verb) = be different. Very (adverb) = extremely. Same sound, different spelling."),
("VRB-211","see – saw – seen – seeing","'See' is a perception (state) verb, so it doesn't usually take -ing.","SEE → SAW → SEEN: three shapes of one eye.","'I see' also means 'I understand'. 'See you!' = goodbye.","Write a sentence about what you can see from your window.",("open your eyes","light enters them","you notice the bird"),"see vs look vs watch","See = notice with your eyes (no effort). Look (at) = turn your eyes on purpose. Watch = look for some time at something moving."),
("VRB-212","look – looked – looked – looking","'Look' needs 'at' before its object.","LOOK has two O's, like two eyes looking.","'Look!' alone is fine. 'Look like' = resemble: 'You look like your father.'","Write a sentence asking a class to look at something.",("hear a sound","turn your eyes towards it","see what it is"),"look vs see","Look = choose to turn your eyes (needs 'at'). See = notice without trying."),
("VRB-213","watch – watched – watched – watching","'Watch' is only for things you see. For sound, use 'listen to'.","WATCH a match: it moves, and you follow it.","'Watch out!' = be careful. A watch is also a wristwatch.","Write a sentence about a show you watch regularly.",("switch on the TV","look at the moving screen for an hour","turn it off"),"watch vs see","See = notice. Watch = look at something moving for some time. 'I saw a film' (completed) / 'I'm watching a film.'"),
("VRB-214","hear – heard – heard – hearing","'Hear' is a perception (state) verb, so it doesn't usually take -ing.","HEAR has 'EAR' inside.","'I hear you' can mean 'I understand how you feel'. 'Did you hear?' introduces news.","Write a sentence about a sound you hear every morning.",("a sound happens","it reaches your ears","you notice it"),"hear vs listen","Hear = notice sound, without trying. Listen (to) = pay attention to sound on purpose."),
("VRB-215","listen – listened – listened – listening","'Listen' needs 'to' before its object.","LISTEN has a silent T. Its letters rearrange to SILENT: you listen in silence.","'Listen!' alone is fine. 'Listen up' = pay attention now.","Write a sentence about the music you listen to while travelling.",("music starts","pay attention to it","enjoy the song"),"listen vs hear","Listen = choose to pay attention (needs 'to'). Hear = sound just reaches you."),
("VRB-216","smell – smelled/smelt – smelled/smelt – smelling","When 'smell' describes a quality, it's a state verb: 'This smells good.'","SMELL has double L, like two nostrils.","'Something smells fishy' = something seems suspicious.","Write a sentence about a smell that reminds you of home.",("food is cooking","the smell reaches your nose","you feel hungry"),"smell good vs smell well","Smell GOOD = has a nice smell (adjective after 'smell'). 'Smell well' is rare and means your nose works well."),
("VRB-217","taste – tasted – tasted – tasting","When 'taste' describes flavour, it's a state verb: 'This tastes good.'","TASTE has 'TA' like the tip of your tongue.","'It tastes of mango' / 'It tastes like mango' are both fine.","Write a sentence about how your favourite dish tastes.",("put food in your mouth","notice its flavour","decide if you like it"),"taste good vs tasty","'It tastes good' (verb + adjective). 'It's tasty' (adjective). Both are correct."),
("VRB-218","touch – touched – touched – touching","After 'don't', use the base verb.","TOUCH: a soft 'ch' at the end, like fingers on a surface.","'Keep in touch' = stay in contact. 'I'm touched' = I'm emotionally moved.","Write a sentence about something children shouldn't touch.",("reach out a hand","put fingers on the surface","feel its texture"),"touch vs feel","Touch = put your hand on something. Feel = notice through touch, or experience an emotion."),
("VRB-219","feel – felt – felt – feeling","For emotions, the simple form is usual. 'I'm feeling' is also common in speech for temporary states.","FEEL → FELT, like KNEEL → KNELT.","'How are you feeling?' is natural when someone is ill.","Write a sentence about how you feel on Monday mornings.",("something happens","emotion rises inside","you say 'I feel happy'"),"feel vs fill","Feel (long 'ee') = experience. Fill (short 'i') = make full."),
("VRB-220","notice – noticed – noticed – noticing","For an observation that continues up to now, the present perfect ('I've noticed') fits better than -ing.","NOTICE: a NOTICE board is where you notice announcements.","'Notice period' = the time you must work after resigning.","Write a sentence about something small you noticed today.",("look around","see something unusual","it catches your attention"),"notice vs see","See = use your eyes. Notice = become aware of something, often small or new."),
("VRB-221","observe – observed – observed – observing","An action continuing from a past time to now needs the present perfect continuous.","OB-SERVE: serve the eye, watch closely.","'Observe' also means follow a rule or festival: 'observe a fast'.","Write a sentence about an animal you have observed carefully.",("set up the experiment","watch every change closely","write notes"),"observe vs watch","Watch = look at for some time. Observe = watch carefully to learn something."),
("VRB-222","stare – stared – stared – staring","'Stare' needs 'at' before its object.","STARE: like looking at a STAR for a long time.","Staring at people is considered rude in many cultures.","Write a sentence about something you once stared at in surprise.",("see something strange","keep looking without blinking","look away at last"),"stare vs glance","Stare = look for a long time. Glance = look very quickly."),
("VRB-223","glance – glanced – glanced – glancing","'Glance' needs 'at' before its object.","GLANCE: like a lance thrown past — quick.","'At a glance' = with one quick look.","Write a sentence about what you glance at before leaving home.",("hold the letter","look at it for a second","put it down"),"glance vs glimpse","Glance = you look quickly on purpose. Glimpse = you see something only for a moment, often by chance."),
("VRB-224","peek – peeked – peeked – peeking","'Peek' usually needs a preposition like 'into', 'at' or 'through'.","PEEK: two E's, like two eyes peeking through a gap.","'Sneak peek' = an early, short look at something.","Write a sentence about a time you peeked at a surprise.",("see a closed box","open it a little and look inside","close it quickly"),"peek vs peak","Peek = a quick secret look. Peak = the top of a mountain, or the highest point."),
("VRB-225","sense – sensed – sensed – sensing","'Sense' is usually used in the simple form, not -ing.","SENSE: your five SENSES tell you things without words.","'It makes sense' = it's logical.","Write a sentence about a time you sensed something was wrong.",("someone is quiet","you notice small signs","you feel something is wrong"),"sense vs feel","Feel = experience an emotion or touch. Sense = become aware of something without being told."),
("VRB-226","perceive – perceived – perceived – perceiving","Use 'perceive someone/something AS…', not without 'as'.","PERCEIVE: 'I before E except after C' — perCEIve.","'Perception' is how people see you: 'public perception'.","Write a sentence about how people perceive your city.",("see how someone acts","form an idea","see them in a certain way"),"perceive vs see","See = notice with your eyes. Perceive = understand or view in a particular way (formal).","I perceived him an honest man.","I perceived him as an honest man."),
("VRB-227","glimpse – glimpsed – glimpsed – glimpsing","As a verb, 'glimpse' takes a direct object, with no 'at'. 'Catch a glimpse of' is also common.","GLIMPSE: a GLIMmer of light — seen for a moment.","'Catch a glimpse of' is the most common pattern.","Write a sentence about a famous person you glimpsed once.",("something passes by","see it for a second","it's gone"),"glimpse vs glance","Glimpse = see briefly, often by chance. Glance = look briefly on purpose."),
("VRB-228","spot – spotted – spotted – spotting","'Spot' takes a direct object, with no 'on'.","SPOT: a small dot you notice on a page.","'On the spot' = immediately, right there.","Write a sentence about a mistake you spotted in a sign or menu.",("read the page","see a small error","point it out"),"spot vs notice","Same idea. Spot is informal and suggests finding something small or hidden."),
("VRB-229","detect – detected – detected – detecting","A finished past action needs -ed: detected.","DETECT → DETECTIVE: someone who detects clues.","'Smoke detector' = a device that notices smoke.","Write a sentence about a machine that detects something.",("something is hidden","use tests or tools","find it"),"detect vs discover","Detect = notice something hard to see (a disease, a signal). Discover = find something for the first time."),
("VRB-230","examine – examined – examined – examining","A finished past action needs -ed: examined.","EXAMINE → EXAM: both mean checking carefully.","A doctor 'examines' a patient; a teacher 'examines' students.","Write a sentence about a time a doctor examined you.",("see a problem","check every part closely","find the cause"),"examine vs check","Check = look quickly to make sure. Examine = look very carefully and in detail."),
("VRB-231","make – made – made – making","'Mistake' goes with 'make', not 'do'.","MAKE = create something new; DO = perform a task. Make a cake; do homework.","Common pairs: make a decision, make money, make friends, make noise.","Write a sentence about something you made by hand.",("gather materials","put them together","something new exists"),"make vs do","Make = create or produce (make tea, make a plan). Do = perform an action or task (do work, do exercise)."),
("VRB-232","build – built – built – building","'Build' is irregular: build – built – built.","BUILD has a silent U: say 'bild'.","'Build' also works for abstract things: 'build trust', 'build a career'.","Write a sentence about a building you'd like to build.",("lay the foundation","add walls and a roof","the house stands"),"build vs make","Build = construct something large, piece by piece (house, bridge). Make = the general word."),
("VRB-233","create – created – created – creating","'Last week' needs the past tense: created.","CREATE → CREATIVE: using your mind to make something new.","'Create an account' is standard on apps and websites.","Write a sentence about something creative you created.",("have an idea","work on it","something new exists"),"create vs make","Make = produce anything. Create = make something new and original."),
("VRB-234","design – designed – designed – designing","A finished past action needs -ed: designed.","DESIGN has a silent G: say 'di-ZINE'.","A 'designer' plans how clothes, websites or buildings look.","Write a sentence about something you would like to design.",("think of the purpose","draw a plan","the plan is ready to build"),"design vs draw","Draw = make a picture. Design = plan how something will look and work."),
("VRB-235","construct – constructed – constructed – constructing","A finished past action needs -ed: constructed.","CON-STRUCT → STRUCTURE: what you construct.","'Under construction' signs mean building work is going on.","Write a sentence about a road or bridge being constructed near you.",("plan the road","workers lay materials","the road is ready"),"construct vs build","Same meaning. Construct is more formal and technical."),
("VRB-236","produce – produced – produced – producing","He/she/it in the simple present needs -s: produces.","PRO-DUCE: verb stress on DUCE. PRO-duce (noun) = fruit and vegetables.","A film 'producer' arranges money and people to make a film.","Write a sentence about what your state produces.",("raw materials go in","the factory works","finished goods come out"),"produce vs product","Produce = verb (make). Product = noun (the thing made)."),
("VRB-237","invent – invented – invented – inventing","A finished past action needs -ed: invented.","INVENT → INVENTOR: someone like Edison.","'Invent' can also mean make up a story: 'He invented an excuse.'","Write a sentence about an invention that changed your life.",("see a problem","think of something new","build a new tool"),"invent vs discover","Invent = make something that didn't exist (the telephone). Discover = find something that already existed (a new planet)."),
("VRB-238","repair – repaired – repaired – repairing","A finished past action needs -ed: repaired.","RE-PAIR: make the PAIR (parts) fit again.","'Under repair' = being fixed.","Write a sentence about something you had repaired recently.",("something breaks","the mechanic fixes it","it works again"),"repair vs fix","Same meaning. Fix is informal; repair is more formal and used for machines and buildings."),
("VRB-239","fix – fixed – fixed – fixing","'Already' goes before the main verb, usually with the present perfect.","FIX has an X — like crossing a problem off the list.","Indian English 'fix a meeting' = arrange it. Standard: 'set up' or 'schedule'.","Write a sentence about something you fixed at home.",("something is broken","repair it","it works again"),"fix vs repair","Fix is informal and wider (fix a problem, fix a date). Repair is for physical things.","I fixed already the problem.","I have already fixed the problem."),
("VRB-240","break – broke – broken – breaking","'Break' is irregular: break – broke – broken.","BREAK → BROKE → BROKEN, like SPEAK → SPOKE → SPOKEN.","'Take a break' = rest. 'Break the news' = tell bad news.","Write a sentence about something you broke by accident.",("drop the phone","the screen hits the floor","the screen cracks"),"break vs brake","Break = separate into pieces. Brake = the part of a vehicle that stops it. Same sound."),
("VRB-241","assemble – assembled – assembled – assembling","'Assemble' already means come together, so 'together' is extra.","AS-SEMBLE: bring things to one place, like a school ASSEMBLY.","'Assembly line' = a factory line where parts are joined.","Write a sentence about furniture you have assembled.",("open the box of parts","join them step by step","the shelf stands"),"assemble vs gather","Gather = collect or come together. Assemble = come together formally, or put parts together.","We assembled together in the hall.","We assembled in the hall."),
("VRB-242","install – installed – installed – installing","After 'need to', use the base verb.","IN-STALL: put a stall (a fixed place) IN.","'Install an app' on a phone; 'install a fan' in a room.","Write a sentence about an app you installed recently.",("download the file","follow the setup steps","the app is ready"),"install vs download","Download = copy the file to your device. Install = set it up so it works."),
("VRB-243","remove – removed – removed – removing","'Remove' already means 'take off/away', so 'off' is extra.","RE-MOVE: MOVE it away.","'Remove your shoes' is common in temples and homes.","Write a sentence about something you remove before sleeping.",("something is on the table","take it away","the table is empty"),"remove vs take off","Take off = remove clothes (informal). Remove is more formal and wider."),
("VRB-244","replace – replaced – replaced – replacing","Use 'replace X WITH Y', not 'by'.","RE-PLACE: put something new in the PLACE of the old.","'Replace' also means be the new person: 'She replaced him as manager.'","Write a sentence about something you replaced this year.",("the old bulb stops working","take it out","put a new one in"),"replace vs substitute","Replace old WITH new. Substitute new FOR old. The order is opposite."),
("VRB-245","operate – operated – operated – operating","A surgeon operates ON a patient. A finished past action also needs -ed.","OPERATE → OPERATION: both surgery and machines.","'Operate' also means run a business: 'The shop operates 24 hours.'","Write a sentence about a machine you can operate.",("switch on the machine","control its buttons","it does its job"),"operate vs use","Use = do something with a tool. Operate = control a machine, or perform surgery."),
("VRB-246","use – used – used – using","A finished past action needs -ed, and after 'to', use the base verb.","USE: 'you' + 'S': how you do something.","'Used to' = a past habit: 'I used to play cricket.'","Write a sentence about something you use every day.",("pick up a tool","do a task with it","put it back"),"use vs used to","Use = do something with a tool. Used to = something you did often in the past but not now."),
("VRB-247","handle – handled – handled – handling","The order is verb + object + adverb: 'handles the stress very well'.","HANDLE has 'HAND': you hold it in your hand.","'Handle with care' is printed on fragile boxes.","Write a sentence about how you handle pressure.",("a problem appears","deal with it calmly","it's under control"),"handle vs manage","Handle = deal with a situation or object. Manage = handle AND control or be in charge of over time."),
("VRB-248","maintain – maintained – maintained – maintaining","After 'to', use the base verb, not -ing.","MAIN-TAIN: keep it in the MAIN (good) condition.","'Maintenance' is the noun (note: maintEnance, not maintAInance).","Write a sentence about how you maintain your bike or phone.",("the machine works well","service it regularly","it keeps working"),"maintain vs keep","Same idea. Maintain is more formal and suggests care and effort."),
("VRB-249","upgrade – upgraded – upgraded – upgrading","After 'to', you need a full noun phrase, like 'a better model'.","UP + GRADE: move up to a better grade.","'Upgrade' is also a noun: 'a free upgrade to business class'.","Write a sentence about something you'd like to upgrade.",("use an old version","choose a newer one","enjoy better features"),"upgrade vs update","Update = add the latest changes. Upgrade = move to a better, higher version."),
("VRB-250","dismantle – dismantled – dismantled – dismantling","A finished past action needs -ed: dismantled.","DIS-MANTLE: take off the 'mantle' (covering) piece by piece.","'Dismantle a system' can also mean end it completely.","Write a sentence about something you dismantled to repair.",("the tent is standing","take it apart piece by piece","pack the pieces away"),"dismantle vs break","Break = damage by force. Dismantle = take apart carefully so it can be rebuilt."),
]
